digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56697-x
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study aims to determine how Mtb PhoP toggles between transcriptional activation and repression by investigating PhoP-dependent RNAP-promoter complexes with different PHO-box architectures using cryo-EM TAC structures and a repression-complex model.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When an important bacterial response regulator has unresolved context-dependent activation and repression mechanisms, structural analysis across promoter architectures can test how protein-DNA and protein-protein interactions determine regulatory output."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) For Mtb PhoP, complex PHO-box promoter architecture and PhoP-DNA contacts are plausible context-dependent features to investigate as determinants of activation versus repression."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Given PhoP’s unresolved dual regulatory mechanism and the plausible role of complex PHO-box promoter architecture, a cryo-EM TAC series plus a repression-complex model is an appropriate integrative plan to test how PhoP switches between activation and repression contexts."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) A PhoP-dependent repression-complex context can be considered when RNAP, PhoP, GlnR, and a cross-talked nitrogen-assimilation promoter are modeled, because repressors can inhibit initiation by impeding RNAP or activators."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) PhoP binding to PHO boxes at promoters makes PhoP-DNA contacts a plausible structural feature to examine in promoter-dependent transcription regulation."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Transcription factors can influence bacterial transcription initiation in promoter-context-dependent ways, including facilitating RPo formation at some promoters and inhibiting RNAP or activators at others."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Dual OmpR/PhoB-family response regulators involved in stress, survival, or pathogenicity are appropriate subjects for context-dependent mechanistic study."];
  "nI_init_conc" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that initiation is orchestrated by RNAP/TFs and that σ recognizes -10/-35 elements, therefore RNAP–promoter interaction surfaces provide mechanistic leverage for regulation at initiation."];
  "nI_init_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If transcription initiation is orchestrated by RNAP/TFs and σ recognizes -10/-35 promoter elements, then initiation depends on specific RNAP–promoter interactions that can be modulated by transcription factors."];
  "nI_integrate_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a regulator is a high-impact dual regulator with an unresolved mechanism and a plausible architecture-dependent hypothesis, then a research plan should test this by structurally resolving activation complexes across architectures and reconstructing a repression complex in a relevant cross-talk context."];
  "nI_ompR_dual" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given observations that many TFs are dual and a common pattern that OmpR/PhoB RRs are largely dual, therefore OmpR/PhoB regulators commonly require context-dependent dual-mechanism explanations."];
  "nI_rpo_conc" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that σ recognizes -10/-35 and conserved contacts promote RPo and that altering such contacts can regulate initiation, therefore probing how factors alter these contacts can explain regulation at initiation."];
  "nI_rpo_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If promoter recognition/open-complex formation (RPo) is the bottleneck step, then factors that alter σ–DNA and RNAP–DNA/protein interactions can strongly regulate transcription initiation."];
  "nS1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently transcription initiation is a key control point of bacterial gene expression and is orchestrated by RNAP plus transcription factors."];
  "nS10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently most OmpR/PhoB-family response regulators (winged-helix DNA-binding) function as dual transcription regulators."];
  "nS12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently two-component systems transiently regulate stress-responsive genes that enhance bacterial survival and pathogenicity."];
  "nS14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently Mtb PhoP regulates >100 genes (virulence/metabolism/stress) and functions as a global activator and repressor."];
  "nS2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently the principal σ factor recognizes promoter -10 and -35 elements via conserved domains (σR2 and σR4)."];
  "nS22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently PhoP binds PHO boxes (direct-repeat units) where each PHO box has two conserved sites (a and b) separated by several nucleotides."];
  "nS3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently additional σ-RNAP and αCTD-related interactions promote formation of the active open complex RPo that initiates transcription."];
  "nS4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently at promoters with suboptimal elements, activators recruit RNAP or bend DNA to compensate weaker σ–DNA interactions and facilitate RPo formation."];
  "nS41" [label="(41,0,0) Currently using prior competition data, PhoP DNA-binding features, and a cryo-EM map, we build a preliminary cryo-EM model of the PhoP-dependent TRC containing RNAP, PhoP, GlnR, and a cross-talked nitrogen assimilation promoter."];
  "nS7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently repressors can inhibit initiation (e.g., occlusion/DNA looping/locking RNAP) to impede RNAP or activators."];
  "nS9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently many transcription factors act as activators at some promoters and repressors at others, implying context-dependent regulation."];
  "nI_rpo_rule" -> "nI_rpo_conc" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "nS3" -> "nI_rpo_conc" [label="deduction-case"];
  "nI_init_rule" -> "nI_init_conc" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "nS2" -> "nI_init_conc" [label="deduction-case"];
  "nS1" -> "nI_init_rule" [label="deduction-case"];
  "nI_rpo_conc" -> "nI_init_rule" [label="deduction-rule"];
